    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d130">

                <pb facs="0208" n="196" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg196"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">747.47H/10–2544: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in New Zealand</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Patton</hi>)<anchor n="32" xml:id="d130-f1"/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wellington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-25T12:00:00+12:00">October 25,
                            1944—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received October 25—7:42 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>429. As provided in the Canberra agreement Evatt and Ford<anchor n="33"
                        xml:id="d130-f2"/> are arriving here this week-end for discussions with New
                    Zealand Prime Minister early next week. Am definitely informed by Secretary for
                    External Affairs that although this may be played up as a formal conference such
                    would be only “window dressing” as it actually will be only talks for a day or
                    two, that is informal exchange of views only with no definite formal agreements
                    anticipated.</p>
                <p>There are no formal agenda, but it is understood that discussions will concern
                    chiefly, (1) Pacific welfare, health, et cetera (2) Dumbarton Oaks
                        proposals,<anchor n="34" xml:id="d130-f3"/> (3) the Australians wish discuss
                    joint schemes of industrial development under article 35 (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">c</hi>) of the Canberra agreement, but I am
                    confidentially and frankly informed New Zealand has no such plans or schemes to
                    discuss so wishes to minimize this.</p>
                <p>Civil aviation was discussed with Minister Drakeford<anchor n="35"
                        xml:id="d130-f4"/> who was here earlier this month and mutual agreement
                    thereon was reaffirmed as outlined in the Canberra agreement. Repeated to
                    Canberra.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Patton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d130-f1" n="32" type="source" xml:id="d130-f1-content">
                <p>Kenneth S. Patton, assigned as Minister to New Zealand March 21, 1944.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d130-f2" n="33" xml:id="d130-f2-content">
                <p>Presumably reference is to F. M. Fordé, Australian Deputy Prime Minister and
                    Minister for the Army.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d130-f3" n="34" xml:id="d130-f3-content">
                <p>For the text of the Dumbarton Oaks Proposals for the Establishment of a General
                    International Organization, see <ref target="frus1944v01">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>
                    </ref>, section entitled “Preliminaries to the establishment of an international
                    organization for the maintenance of international peace and security,” part
                    II.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d130-f4" n="35" xml:id="d130-f4-content">
                <p>A. S. Drakeford, Australian Minister for Air and Civil Aviation.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
